# Five lines to your first plot
prompt: "What is the shortest script that produces a real result: a mean, a table and a histogram of wage income by sex? Why does every script have to begin with `require no.ssb.fdb:... as db` and `create-dataset`? Where does the result appear if you never get to see a single individual's data?"
next: {"title":"What is actually inside the datastore","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/microdata/what-is-actually-inside-the-datastore.cast"}

## Five lines to your first plot — the whole script
voice: female
level: basic

    ```microdata md width 960 show below lines 6 frame screen marks ["no.ssb.fdb:54","as db","create-dataset persons","histogram wage"] draw.mode type hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    histogram wage
    ```

Over these four short parts we will write one complete microdata script — five lines that turn Norway's register data into a real result. This is line one.
    draw md md_line_1 parallel true

    pause 1

The second line opens an empty dataset called persons: a blank spreadsheet with one row for every person, and no columns yet.
    draw md_line_2

    pause 1

The third line fills the first column — sex, for everyone in the population register.
    draw md_line_3

The fourth adds wage income as it stood on the first of January twenty twenty-two, because income belongs to a date, so you always give one.
    draw md_line_4

Our question for the whole course lives in that column: the roughly fifty thousand people born in Norway in 1985 turned thirty-seven that year, and we want to know what the men among them earned and what the women earned.
    point at.ref md_line_4 gesture underline

    pause 1

And the fifth line asks for a picture: a histogram of those wages. That is the entire program.
    draw md_line_5

Notice what is missing — no file to download, no path, no cleaning. The data never comes to you; Statistics Norway keeps it behind a wall, and your script travels there instead.
    highlight md effect glow

    wait

Line one is the handshake: it names the datastore you are asking, and pins its version — fifty-four here.
    draw md_mark_1

    pause 1

Pinning the version is not bureaucracy — variables get revised — and db is simply the nickname every later line uses when it reaches for one.
    draw md_mark_2

Line two makes the table those variables land in; leave it out and the import has nowhere to go, so microdata stops before it starts.
    draw md_mark_3

Those two lines are the commonest reason a beginner's script refuses to run, which is why part two is about nothing else.

    wait

    erase md_mark_1 md_mark_2 md_mark_3 parallel true

Now run it. Back comes the distribution of wage income, every person in the dataset counted into a bar.
    draw md_out

So where is the data? You never see it. What returns is counted and grouped — a chart, a table, a mean — and never anyone's row.
    point at.ref md_out gesture circle delivery grave

    pause 1

And only this last line changes: summarize for the mean, tabulate for a table by sex, while the four lines above stay exactly as they are.
    draw md_mark_4

    pause 1

Five lines — name the database, make a dataset, import two columns, ask a question — and the register answers you without ever showing you a single person.
    highlight md effect glow

Have a go: swap that last line and run it yourself.
    explore code md

## Why the first two lines
voice: female
level: basic

    ```microdata md x 500 y 608 width 700 font_size 18 show code frame screen marks ["no.ssb.fdb:54","as db","create-dataset persons"] draw.mode type hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    ```
    shape vault #2f6b8f shape rect x 250 y 250 width 320 height 250 hidden true
    text t1 "BEFOLKNING_KJOENN" x 250 y 310 font_size 20 hidden true
    text t2 "INNTEKT_WLONN" x 250 y 250 font_size 20 hidden true
    text t3 "NUDB_BU" x 250 y 190 font_size 20 hidden true
    label l_vault "no.ssb.fdb — the datastore" above #2f6b8f attach_to vault hidden true
    label l_ver "version 54" below #8a5fa8 attach_to vault hidden true
    shape tbl dashed #b5482e shape rect x 760 y 250 width 300 height 180 hidden true
    text empty_txt "no rows, no columns" #8f887c x 760 y 250 font_size 20 hidden true
    label l_tbl "persons" above #b5482e attach_to tbl hidden true
    arrow arrow_import dashed #8f887c from.anchor right from.ref vault to.anchor left to.ref tbl hidden true
    label l_arrow "lines 3–5: import" above #8f887c attach_to arrow_import hidden true
    group desk members ["vault","t1","t2","t3","tbl","empty_txt","arrow_import"] fit.h 330 fit.w 880 fit.x 60 fit.y 60 hidden true

Now that we have seen the whole five-line script, let us slow down on the two lines that print nothing — and that decide whether anyone can ever reproduce your result.
    draw md md_line_1 parallel true

The first says which register archive we are talking to; the second makes an empty table to put things in.
    draw md_line_2

    pause 1

Think of the datastore as a locked archive of Statistics Norway's registers — everyone in the country, and you are never let inside.
    draw vault l_vault

On its shelves sit the variables under their real, rather shouty names: sex, wage income, highest completed education.
    draw t1 t2 t3 parallel true

    pause 1

So the first line is an address — the population and income database — and the number after the colon is its version.
    draw md_mark_1

And this gives that address a nickname, db, so every later line can simply say db slash the variable it wants.
    draw md_mark_2

    wait

The second line puts an empty table on your own desk and names it persons.
    draw tbl l_tbl

It really is empty — no rows, no columns, not one number in it yet.
    draw empty_txt

You have to say this out loud, because everything that follows — import, keep, summarize — quietly works on the table you made last.
    draw md_mark_3

Leave it out, and the script stops at line three with nowhere to pour the data.
    highlight tbl effect glow

    pause 1

    wait

Now the detail beginners skip: the version number, here fifty-four.
    draw l_ver

Registers get revised — an income year is corrected, a variable redefined — and a new version of the datastore is published.
    highlight l_ver effect pulse

Pin the version, and the same script returns the same mean wage for the 1985 cohort to the last krone, today or three years from now.
    point at.ref md_line_1 gesture underline

Which is why a journal or a co-author asks for your script rather than your data: with the version pinned, a register analysis can be checked by someone who will never see a single person in it.
    delivery grave

    ask "Click the part of the figure that holds the raw register data." intro "One quick check." widget click answer vault
        right: The archive — your own table starts out empty and stays empty until line three pours something into it.
        wrong: That is the table you created; the registers themselves sit in the archive on the left.

Only then do the next three lines carry a copy of a couple of columns across, from the archive into your table.
    draw arrow_import l_arrow

    pause 1

So the two silent lines decide everything: which archive, which version — and which table the rest of the script is quietly filling.

## Three lines that produce results
voice: female
level: basic

    ```microdata md width 960 font_size 16 show below lines 6 frame screen marks ["BEFOLKNING_KJOENN","2022-01-01","summarize wage"] draw.mode type hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    summarize wage
    ```

Now that we have seen why every script begins by naming the datastore and creating an empty dataset, let us add the lines that actually produce something.
    draw md md_line_1 md_line_2 parallel true

    pause 1

The third line reaches into the datastore and pulls out one variable: sex, for every person in the population.
    draw md_line_3

The capitals are the variable's real name in the register — and the little word after it, as sex, is the short nickname you will type for the rest of the script.
    draw md_mark_1

    pause 1

The fourth line does the same for money: wage income, the pay reported to the tax authorities, imported here under the nickname wage.
    draw md_line_4

This date is not decoration — income is a fact about a particular year, so the script has to say which year you are asking about.
    draw md_mark_2

That is the classic register mistake, by the way: reading a 2022 income onto a population counted in 2015, and never noticing the mismatch.
    highlight md_line_4 effect glow

    pause 1

    wait

And now the fifth line, which is the entire analysis: summarize wage.
    draw md_line_5

    pause 1

Back comes a table — how many people, the average, the spread — all of it counted on the server, over everyone in the dataset.
    draw md_out

    pause 1

And notice what did not come back: no rows, no names, no single person's pay. You asked for a summary, and a summary is what you were handed.
    focus md_out

    ask "Click the line that brought wage income into the dataset." widget click answer md_line_4
        intro: A quick check before we go on.
        right: The fourth line — the one importing INNTEKT_WLONN under the nickname wage, for the first of January 2022.
        wrong: Not that one — look for the line that names an income variable and a date.

    wait

Here is the rhythm worth remembering: everything above stays, and this last line is the only one you change.
    draw md_mark_3

    pause 1

Write tabulate sex instead, and you get a table of how many women and men there are. Write histogram wage, and you get your first plot. One line, one output.

Try it yourself — swap that last line and run the script again.
    explore code md

    pause 1

Two lines to fetch, one line to ask: in microdata your analysis is built one verb at a time, and every verb hands back a summary — never a person.
    delivery grave

## Where the result appears
voice: female
level: basic

    ```microdata md width 900 font_size 16 show below lines 5 frame screen hidden true
    require no.ssb.fdb:54 as db
    create-dataset persons
    import db/BEFOLKNING_KJOENN as sex
    import db/INNTEKT_WLONN 2022-01-01 as wage
    histogram wage
    ```
    shape laptop shape rect x 220 y 400 width 200 height 140 hidden true
    shape server shape rect x 700 y 400 width 280 height 330 hidden true
    pieces rows #8f887c x 700 y 390 width 210 height 230 n 2 rows 5 of grid hidden true
    icon lock size 56 x 700 y 580 of lock hidden true
    arrow arrow_out laptop -> server curved #2f6b8f hidden true
    arrow arrow_back server -> laptop curved #b5482e hidden true
    group flyt members ["laptop","server","rows","lock","arrow_out","arrow_back"] fit.h 420 fit.w 820 fit.x 90 fit.y 170 hidden true
    label l_laptop "Your browser" below attach_to laptop hidden true
    label l_server "The datastore, on a server" right attach_to server hidden true
    label l_out "your five lines" above #2f6b8f attach_to arrow_out hidden true
    label l_back "counts, means, bar heights" below #b5482e attach_to arrow_back hidden true

Now that we have seen the three lines that produce a result, the last question is where that result appears — because if you look for a data window here, you will never find one.
    draw md md_line_1 md_line_2 parallel true

    pause 1

These two lines fetch what we need: each person's sex, and their wage income as it stood on the first of January, twenty twenty-two.
    draw md_line_3 md_line_4

    pause 1

And the fifth line asks for a histogram of those wages. Click when you are ready to run it.
    draw md_line_5

    wait

There it is: your first plot, drawn from the wages of about two and a half million employed Norwegians.
    draw md_out

    pause 1

Each bar is a count of people — how many earned between this much and that much. Not one of those people is on your screen.
    point at.ref md_out gesture circle

    wait

Which is strange, if you think about it. So let us follow where the script actually went.
    clear

Here is your machine: a browser tab with an editor in it. No files, no data folder, nothing to save.
    draw laptop l_laptop

    pause 1

The data sits somewhere else — a server at Statistics Norway, behind a door you will never walk through.
    draw server lock l_server

    pause 1

Inside are the rows: one line per person, every resident of Norway, with their sex, their income, their municipality.
    draw rows

You cannot open a row, scroll it, print it or download it — and that is precisely why anyone is allowed near this data without an application taking months.
    focus rows

    pause 1

What leaves your browser is not data. It is instructions: five lines of text, sent off to be run over there.
    draw arrow_out l_out

    pause 1

And what comes back is not data either — a mean, a table of counts, the heights of ten bars.
    draw arrow_back l_back

Script out, aggregates back — that round trip happens every single time you press run.
    flow arrow_out arrow_back

    wait

    clear

So the output panel is not a window onto your data. It is the only form your data will ever take for you.
    show md md_line_1 md_line_2 md_line_3 md_line_4 md_line_5 md_out

Which flips the usual habit: in Stata or R you inspect rows first and trust them; here you must ask the output the questions you would have answered by eye.
    highlight md_out effect glow

    pause 1

    quiz "Where does your microdata script actually run?"
        intro: One quick check before we close the series.
        right: It runs on the server: your five lines travel out, and only aggregated results travel back.
        wrong: Nothing is ever downloaded — the individual rows never leave the server.
        * In your browser, on data you downloaded
        + On the server — only aggregates come back
        * On a local copy you keep on your laptop

    pause 1

Five lines gave you a population-wide plot without ever seeing a person — and every lecture from here just adds one more line to this same script, with the answer always arriving the same way: computed there, returned as an aggregate, read in that panel.
    delivery grave

## Where next
end_page: true

